(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-arterial-occlusive-aneurysm-quiz"]={"title":"Arterial Occlusive Disease and Aortic Aneurysm Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Why is vascular imaging usually skipped for an irreversibly ischemic limb?","choices":["Pulses are still palpable","The limb recovers without care","Amputation is needed regardless","Contrast would worsen the limb"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Vascular imaging is generally not immediately needed for limbs that are irreversibly damaged, because nonviable extremities require major amputation regardless of therapy. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"How are most thoracic aortic aneurysms found?","choices":["By blue toe syndrome","Incidentally on chest radiograph","By sudden tearing chest pain","By a pulsatile abdominal mass"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Most thoracic aortic aneurysms are asymptomatic and found incidentally on chest radiograph; a history of hypertension is common, and symptoms depend on location. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"Where is ischemic rest pain usually localized?","choices":["Calf muscle","Buttock and hip","Front of the thigh","Forefoot and toes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Ischemic rest pain is constant, throbbing, burning or shooting pain with cool feet, typically in the forefoot and toes, the most distal part of the extremity. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What defines acute limb ischemia?","choices":["Sudden occlusion of a patent artery","Gradual narrowing over decades","Pain in calf muscles with walking","Swelling from blocked leg veins"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Acute limb ischemia is sudden occlusion of a previously patent artery, with abrupt severe pain and absent pulses in the involved extremity; it is a vascular emergency. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"Which procedure is the gold standard for high-grade carotid stenosis?","choices":["Carotid bypass grafting","Carotid endarterectomy","Carotid angioplasty with stenting","Antiplatelet therapy alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Carotid endarterectomy removes plaque from inside the artery through a surgical incision and is the gold standard for high-grade stenosis; its complications are stroke or nerve damage. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"Which study is the choice for initial abdominal aortic aneurysm screening?","choices":["Conventional angiography","Magnetic resonance imaging","Abdominal ultrasound","Abdominal X-ray"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Abdominal ultrasound is the diagnostic study of choice for initial screening for an aneurysm and is used for serial documentation of its size. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"Which patient should not undergo magnetic resonance angiography?","choices":["One with contrast allergy","One with renal impairment","One with diabetes","One with a pacemaker"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Magnetic resonance angiography is contraindicated with pacemakers and intracranial aneurysm clips; it is the alternative to computed tomography angiography when contrast allergy is present. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which complication can a nonhealing ischemic toe ulcer lead to?","choices":["Osteomyelitis","Aortic dissection","Carotid stenosis","Pseudoaneurysm"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nonhealing ischemic ulcers are painful, begin as minor trauma, fail to heal because supply cannot meet the demands of healing tissue, and can lead to osteomyelitis. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What may the electrocardiogram show in aortic dissection?","choices":["Diagnostic Q waves","It may be normal","Always ST elevation","Always low voltage"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The electrocardiogram may be normal in aortic dissection, so the diagnosis rests on computed tomography of the chest and abdomen, magnetic resonance imaging or transesophageal echocardiography. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 117"},{"q":"Which condition contraindicates computed tomography angiography?","choices":["A prior bypass graft","Decreased renal function","A cardiac pacemaker","Intracranial aneurysm clips"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Computed tomography angiography uses contrast, so contrast allergy and decreased renal function contraindicate it; magnetic resonance angiography is the alternative when contrast allergy is the problem. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which history is present in most cases of aortic dissection?","choices":["Recent cardiac catheterization","Diabetes","Recent viral illness","Hypertension"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A history of hypertension is present in most cases of aortic dissection, which occurs mainly in the 60s and 70s and in males more than females. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 115"},{"q":"Which test is the most widely used initial screening tool for carotid artery disease?","choices":["Plain neck radiography","Duplex ultrasonography","Digital subtraction angiography","Magnetic resonance angiography"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Duplex ultrasonography is the most widely used initial screening tool and is also used to monitor patients serially for progression or after intervention. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"Where do ischemic ulcers of critical limb ischemia usually appear?","choices":["Toe tips and between digits","Inner ankle above the heel","Front of the lower shin","Back of the calf"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Ulcers form where arterial branches terminate, at the tips of the toes and between digits, and at areas of increased focal pressure such as the metatarsal heads. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"When is surgery advised for asymptomatic carotid stenosis?","choices":["Over 80% and life expectancy over 5 years","Over 50% and life expectancy over 1 year","Over 70% and life expectancy over 2 years","Over 90% at any life expectancy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Asymptomatic patients are advised to have surgery with more than 80% stenosis and a life expectancy of more than 5 years; symptomatic patients qualify above 70%. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Which imaging is most widely used for thoracic aortic aneurysm?","choices":["Magnetic resonance angiography","Duplex ultrasonography","Computed tomography angiography","Abdominal ultrasound"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Computed tomography angiography is the most widely used study, visualizing the entire thoracic and abdominal aorta; magnetic resonance angiography is time consuming. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"Which antiplatelet agents are used in medical management of carotid disease?","choices":["Esmolol or nitroprusside","Pravastatin or simvastatin","Aspirin or clopidogrel","Heparin or alteplase"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Antiplatelet therapy with aspirin or clopidogrel prevents platelet aggregation, alongside antihypertensives, glycemic control, statins and smoking cessation. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"What defines aortic dissection?","choices":["A cavity outside the vessel wall","Dilation of all three layers","Plaque narrowing of the lumen","An intimal tear with a false lumen"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Aortic dissection forms from an intimal tear typically contained by the media; the wall layers separate, creating a false lumen within the media where blood can pool. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 114"},{"q":"Isolated groin pain from an expanding abdominal aortic aneurysm reflects pressure on which nerve?","choices":["Femoral nerve","Sciatic nerve","Phrenic nerve","Recurrent laryngeal nerve"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A symptomatic, unruptured aneurysm can cause abdominal, back or flank pain, pain radiating to the low back, and isolated groin pain from pressure on the femoral nerve. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"Which group has the highest incidence of abdominal aortic aneurysm?","choices":["Caucasian men","Hispanic women","Asian men","African American women"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Caucasian men have the highest incidence; aneurysm is uncommon in African American, Asian and Hispanic populations, and men outnumber women 2:1 under 80. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"Which medical therapy is started in acute limb ischemia?","choices":["Oral pentoxifylline","Intravenous heparin","Oral cilostazol","Oral pravastatin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Medical therapy for acute peripheral arterial occlusion is anticoagulation with intravenous heparin, alongside an immediate vascular surgery consult. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"In darker skin, which signs help replace color change when judging blood flow?","choices":["Temperature, texture, capillary refill","Pulse rate, blood pressure, weight","Sweating, nail color, hair color","Itch, bruising, swelling"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pallor and redness can be harder to see in darker skin, so temperature, texture (rough, scaly, dry or atrophic skin, hair loss) and capillary refill time serve as additional indicators. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm is often misdiagnosed as which condition?","choices":["Carotid stenosis","Critical limb ischemia","Peripheral neuropathy","Renal colic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Ruptured aneurysm is often misdiagnosed as renal colic, diverticulitis, gastrointestinal hemorrhage or ischemic bowel, because the pain sits in the abdomen, back or flank. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"Failure of which structural proteins underlies aortic aneurysm?","choices":["Keratin and collagen of the intima","Actin and myosin of the adventitia","Fibrin and platelets of the lumen","Elastin and collagen of the media"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Aortic aneurysm reflects failure of the major structural proteins, elastin and collagen, in the media; the cause is unclear, with genetic predisposition and possible mechanical weakening by atherosclerosis. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"Which plaque is the vulnerable, unstable type?","choices":["Calcified core, thick media layer","Fatty core, thin fibrous shell","Small core, thick fibrous shell","Fatty streak, no fibrous shell"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A vulnerable plaque has a fatty core under a thin fibrous outer shell and can rupture into the bloodstream; clotting then begins at the rupture site and can go on to occlude the artery. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which syndrome results when atheroemboli occlude the digital arteries?","choices":["Carotid steal syndrome","Compartment syndrome","Blue toe syndrome","Leriche syndrome"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Digital vascular occlusion may cause ischemia and blue toe syndrome, with localized tenderness, pallor and livedo reticularis at the embolization sites; necrosis and gangrene may develop. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Which cause of leg pain is often present at rest and worsened by exercise?","choices":["Hip or knee osteoarthritis","Intermittent claudication","Venous insufficiency","Peripheral neuropathy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Osteoarthritis of the hip or knee is often present at rest and exacerbated by exercise, and the pain may improve when weight is taken off the joint. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What is the most common predisposing factor for aortic dissection?","choices":["Embolism from atrial fibrillation","Destruction of the carotid bulb","Medial collagen and elastin degeneration","Laceration by an access needle"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The most common predisposing factor is degeneration of medial collagen and elastin, with Marfan syndrome the classic medial degeneration. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 115"},{"q":"Catheter-directed thrombolysis is indicated for acute limb ischemia of what duration?","choices":["Under 24 hours","Under 6 months","Under 3 hours","Under 14 days"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Intra-arterial catheter-directed thrombolysis, with alteplase as the thrombolytic, is indicated for acute limb ischemia of less than 14 days' duration. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Which pseudoaneurysms may be managed by watching and waiting?","choices":["Those in the aorta","Those causing nerve pain","Those under 2 cm","Those over 5 cm"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A pseudoaneurysm under 2 cm in diameter may undergo spontaneous thrombosis and regression, so watch and wait; otherwise an ultrasound-guided thrombin injection seals the leak. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 113"},{"q":"What relieves spinal stenosis pain but not claudication?","choices":["Slowing the walking pace","Sitting or leaning forward","Standing still briefly","Raising the legs"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Spinal stenosis gives claudication-like symptoms with numbness and weakness, relieved by rest and by sitting down or leaning forward to straighten the lumbar spine. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 41"}]};
